Done. The file is at `/Users/user/ErrorBoundary.tsx`.

**What it does:**

- `getDerivedStateFromError` — captures the error into state, triggering the fallback render
- `componentDidCatch` — logs `error.message` and the component stack to `console.error`, then calls the optional `onError` prop for external logging (Sentry, etc.)
- `reset()` — clears error state so React re-mounts the children on retry
- `DefaultFallback` — a minimal accessible (`role="alert"`) UI with the error message and a **Try again** button

**Usage:**

```tsx
// Basic — uses built-in fallback UI
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary
  fallback={(error, reset) => (
    <MyCustomUI message={error.message} onRetry={reset} />
  )}
  onError={(error, info) => Sentry.captureException(error, { extra: info })}
>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

**Key design decisions:**
- `fallback` prop accepts a render function (not a node) so it can receive both the `error` and the `reset` callback
- `onError` is optional — the boundary logs to console regardless, so callers only need it for external services
- Inline styles keep the component self-contained with no CSS dependency
